note(s,"""**Page:** manuscript XV, archive number 42. Two physical paper areas meet across the lower third. Four wordless vertical marginal strokes are retained. No date or title. H has only a synopsis.

**043-N1 — Disjoint neighborhoods.** The page says that two compact subsets A and B of R² have disjoint neighborhoods. It does not write the necessary hypothesis A∩B=∅. The literal general claim is false (take A=B={0}). **043-N1-refined:** disjoint compact subsets have disjoint neighborhoods, since their positive minimum distance allows disjoint distance neighborhoods. No missing word was inserted.

**043-N2 — Detour for U.** The proof chooses x″ and y″ near first/last crossings, then joins them near Γx′y′. Condition II supplies connectedness of V∩ext Λ², which is the region needed for the joining arc. Merely saying Λx″y″⊂V and avoiding Γ′ss′, as the page does, does not by itself state avoidance of the inside of Λ². The strengthened choice in that connected open exterior part would address this specific gap, provided the other-arc exclusion has the intended meaning. Lemme I is invoked but is outside these supplied pages, so its content was not independently checked.

**043-N3 — V and written slips.** The lower proof starts “Prouvons que V est connexe” but then writes x,y∈U with the formula for V. It also puts a connecting arc inside ext Λ¹ when the endpoints were allowed in int Λ²∪]s,s′[. An endpoint z∈]s,s′[ lies on Λ¹ and cannot belong to ext Λ¹, so this latter assertion is false literally. **043-N3-refined:** use the intended region int Λ²∪]s,s′[ for this case; its path-connectedness needs the local boundary argument. The final glued arc is visibly Λzz, not the expected Λzy. Γss′ at the top and Γ′ss′ later are preserved separately; the neighborhood letter V is reused. Dots over x and y are preserved, with no inferred meaning.

**043-N4 — Openness conclusion.** Components of an open subset of R² are open: each point has a small connected ball in the open set, wholly within its component. However, connectedness of two disjoint sets covering the complement alone would not yet prove that they are its components; separation/openness must also be established. This is a logical caution about the written conclusion, not a change to it.

No authorial question or explicit reservation is written. The ending reads c.q.s.d.; no expansion or grammatical correction was supplied.""")
